      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think `mremap()` can copy the pages it moves. Upstream only moves the PTEs, so the pages behind the range stay the same, and anything that pinned them for DMA, such as a driver, io_uring fixed buffers ..., keeps working on the memory the process sees. After a copy, a device holding a pin on the old folios would keep reading and writing memory that the process no longer maps. The comment this patch removes from `mm_mremap_fixed_keeps_slice()` makes the same point.\n\nThe `folio_maybe_dma_pinned()` check in `p3s_mremap_prepare()` only turns the `FOLL_PIN` case into an `-EBUSY` that `mremap()` doesn\u0027t otherwise return, after `mremap_to()` has already unmapped an `MREMAP_FIXED` target. References taken with plain `get_user_pages()` don\u0027t show up in it at all.\n\nThe message doesn\u0027t name a user either. As the removed comment notes, ART\u0027s `MREMAP_FIXED` users (`MarkCompact::KernelPrepareRangeForUffd()` and `MemMap::ReplaceWith()`) keep the slice. The only other callers I found were tests and the exec stack move in aosp/4367983, which has since left the series.\n\nCould we keep the `-EINVAL` from aosp/4312522 for VMAs whose slices follow their address (`!vm_ops \u0026\u0026 !vm_file`, as in `vma_slice_offset()`), and drop the repacking? The `copy_vma()` change alone would still let file, shmem and private `/dev/zero` mappings move to another slice, since their pages don\u0027t change. Tests in aosp/4367986 that move anonymous memory to another slice, such as `hole_anon_p3s` and `packed_swap_p3s`, would then expect `-EINVAL`. I have the same concern on the copy path that aosp/4367997 adds for UFFDIO_MOVE.",
      "isAi": true,
      "revId": "e18c1fcd04a4a8bb9444482f9172d48ac7eaf018",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
